@okwasniewski done in b92ca107, thanks for the pointer. `decisionExecutor` now takes any AI SDK evaluation model and decides through `experimental_evaluate`:

```ts
import { typeSafeAi } from '@ai-sdk/typesafe-ai';

decisionExecutor({ model: typeSafeAi.evaluationModel('jev-latest') });
```

Each decision is one `experimental_evaluate` call with one choice question and `maxRetries: 0`, so the runner's call budget still counts provider requests exactly. `ai` is an optional peer and only loads on that path. It also works the other way: `evaluationModel(clef(...))` exposes the built-in transports as evaluation models, since Cloudflare's Clef has no AI SDK provider yet.

Wiring up the official provider surfaced two details. TypeSafe rounds probabilities to two places, so validation now uses the same half-unit tolerance as the AI SDK. Confidence lives in `providerMetadata.typesafe.confidence`, and the executor reads it from there.

On verification: the official provider is covered against a stubbed TypeSafe response (I don't have TypeSafe credits). Live, the testbed todo flow passed 3/3 with full Clef through `experimental_evaluate`, with the same calls and tokens as the direct transport.

One question: now that Jev runs through `@ai-sdk/typesafe-ai`, would you rather I drop the hand-rolled `jev()` transport and keep only `clef()` and `systemOne()` for endpoints without an AI SDK provider? I kept it for now so this push doesn't remove anything.

fix(oauth): listed enabled Copilot model still fails on the Responses route

## Summary

Updated reproduction for #745 on the current published `e2e@0.17.0`. `e2e models github-copilot` lists `gpt-6-luna` as `GPT-6 Luna  OpenAI, tools, vision`, without `not enabled` or `no chat or responses`. Passing that id to `copilot()` fails the first uncached agent call with `MODEL_PROVIDER_FAILED: the model provider failed: The requested model is not supported.` The same click-only fixture and stored login pass with `gpt-4o`.

This is a follow-up on the same listing-versus-call behavior, so I am adding evidence here instead of opening a duplicate. The adapter now selects `/responses`, and a dispatched request to that endpoint still receives HTTP 400. The previous unconditional `/chat/completions` diagnosis in #778 does not explain this observation by itself. The underlying cause remains unknown.

## Steps to reproduce

Prerequisite: an existing `e2e login github-copilot` login whose model listing includes unmarked `gpt-6-luna`. These steps were run in a disposable consumer of the published packages, on Node `v22.23.2`. Both model provider peers were installed.

1. Install `e2e@0.17.0`, `@e2e-dev/web@0.12.0`, `ai@7.0.127`, `@ai-sdk/openai@4.0.83`, and `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible@3.0.62` as development dependencies. The verification install used `npm install --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund` with those versions pinned in `package.json`. Ensure the web engine's Chromium is installed.
2. Set `E2E_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1` for every e2e command. Run `npx e2e models github-copilot` and confirm the line below has neither unavailable marker.
3. Save these three files:

`server.mjs`:

```js
import http from 'node:http';
const html = `<!doctype html><button onclick="document.getElementById('c').textContent='Clicked'">Click me</button><p id="c">Not clicked</p>`;
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' });
  res.end(html);
}).listen(Number(process.env.PORT), '127.0.0.1');
```

`e2e.config.ts`:

```ts
import type { E2EConfig } from 'e2e';
import { web } from '@e2e-dev/web';
import { copilot } from 'e2e/oauth/copilot';
export default {
  workers: 1, retries: 0, timeout: 60_000,
  agents: { default: {
    model: copilot(process.env.COPILOT_MODEL ?? 'gpt-6-luna'),
    maxModelCalls: 4, maxSteps: 5,
  } },
  targets: [{ engine: web(), app: {
    url: 'http://127.0.0.1:0',
    command: { executable: 'node', args: ['server.mjs'], env: { PORT: '{port}' } },
  } }],
} satisfies E2EConfig;
```

`tests/click.e2e.ts`:

```ts
import { test } from '@e2e-dev/web';
import { expect } from 'e2e';
test('click the button', async ({ app, agent, browser }) => {
  await app.open('/');
  await agent.act('Click the "Click me" button');
  await expect(browser.locator('#c')).toHaveText('Clicked');
});
```

4. Run `COPILOT_MODEL=gpt-6-luna npx e2e run --no-cache --trace off`. It fails at `agent.act` with exit code 3.
5. Run `COPILOT_MODEL=gpt-4o npx e2e run --no-cache --trace off` with the same login. It passes with exit code 0 and two model calls.
6. To check the actual route, prepend this observation-only wrapper to the config and repeat step 4. It forwards the original request unchanged and records only endpoint paths and status codes, never headers, credentials, request bodies, or response bodies.

```ts
const originalFetch = globalThis.fetch;
globalThis.fetch = async (input, init) => {
  const request = input instanceof Request ? input : new Request(input, init);
  const path = new URL(request.url).pathname;
  const response = await originalFetch(input, init);
  if (path === '/models' || path === '/responses' || path === '/chat/completions') {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({ requestPath: path, status: response.status }));
  }
  return response;
};
```

## Expected behavior

The version-matched subscription docs describe the listing as ids to pass to `copilot()`, mark models it cannot call with `not enabled` or `no chat or responses`, and describe endpoint selection from that same listing. An account-enabled unmarked id served through a supported endpoint should be usable through the documented constructor. If the provider rejects it despite that metadata, the workflow should accurately explain the remaining availability restriction rather than present it as an ordinary usable choice.

## Actual behavior

The simplified fixture failed in both local `gpt-6-luna` runs, once without instrumentation and once with the wrapper. Both attempts ended with `MODEL_PROVIDER_FAILED`, exit code 3, and completed cleanup. The agent made no UI actions. The separate `gpt-4o` control passed once with two model calls and one UI action. These counts describe this bounded sample only.

Filtered listing:

```text
gpt-6-luna  GPT-6 Luna  OpenAI, tools, vision
gpt-4o     GPT-4o  Azure OpenAI, tools, vision
```

Instrumented failing run:

```text
{"requestPath":"/models","status":200}
{"requestPath":"/responses","status":400}
MODEL_PROVIDER_FAILED: the model provider failed: The requested model is not supported.
Tests 1 failed | 0 passed (1)
```

A separate public-constructor route probe fetched the model listing and stopped before dispatching a model request. Its filtered `gpt-6-luna` entry reported `policy.state: "enabled"`, `supported_endpoints: ["/responses", "ws:/responses"]`, and `tool_calls: true`, `vision: true`. That probe selected `/responses`; it did not test whether the request would succeed. The instrumented agent run above supplies the actual dispatched HTTP 400 evidence.

## Evidence

- Expected source: `e2e@0.17.0` installed `docs/subscriptions.mdx`, "Pick a model"; current source [docs/subscriptions.mdx](https://github.com/tester-army/e2e/blob/4bfc1aae9bb02465245f10e652f4b27db92409ad/docs/subscriptions.mdx#L133) describes the same markers and endpoint selection.
- Failure source: the inline click fixture's two uncached local runs on the pinned published packages, with the error and endpoint/status output reproduced above. The `gpt-4o` run is the control on the same fixture and login.
- Evidence provenance: observed
- Local verification: reproduced
- Reproduction completeness: complete

The listing, model metadata, two failures, dispatched `/responses` HTTP 400, and passing control were verified locally. Earlier supplied consumer runs also failed for `gpt-6-luna` and passed for `gpt-4o`; the new click-only runs are the primary evidence here.

The published versions remain the latest npm releases checked for this follow-up. Current source at `4bfc1aae9bb02465245f10e652f4b27db92409ad` includes protocol selection in `packages/e2e/src/oauth/copilot.ts` and `copilotProtocolFor` in `packages/e2e/src/oauth/providers/github-copilot.ts`. I did not build or run the repository source; the runtime observations use the published artifacts.

## Restoration check

With the same stored login and fresh listing, the unmarked enabled `gpt-6-luna` selection should complete the click test through `copilot()` under `--no-cache`, or the workflow should identify its actual availability restriction before suggesting it as usable. The same `gpt-4o` control must continue to pass. Merely selecting `/responses` does not satisfy this check, since this reproduction already dispatched a request there and received HTTP 400. Determine the remaining restriction from the known failing request rather than assuming the old endpoint-selection cause.

